      \field{labeltitlesource}{title}
      \field{abstract}{Today's vehicles run various safety-critical applications requiring data input from diverse in-vehicle components. Adaptive Cruise Control (ACC), for example, can rely on the data input from components such as lidar, radar, GNSS, and cameras. Malicious manipulation of any of this data compromises the data integrity and can result in safety incidents or accidents on the road. Security mechanisms like intrusion detection can be in place; however, they can not reliably assess the consequences of attacks on a system level or for arbitrary subsystems. In this paper, we present a Trust Assessment Framework (TAF) that allows an in-vehicle application in a complex System-of-Systems to assess whether it can trust the integrity of its input data. The TAF assesses the trustworthiness of every component in the data flow chain based on collected evidence. We explain this concept with the example of ACC and showcase two possible implementations of the TAF inside a vehicle.}
      \field{booktitle}{21th escar {Europe} : The {World}'s {Leading} {Automotive} {Cyber} {Security} {Conference} ({Hamburg}, 15. - 16.11.2023)}
      \field{title}{In-vehicle trust assessment framework}
